THE LID TABLET'S USB-C SERVICE BUDGET (a PROPOSAL) AND OPTIONS A AND B WITH IT (tablet.py, stream l3batt, MESHSAT-1357).
PROTOTYPE DESIGN: nothing built, bought, powered or measured. The budget is a proposal measurable at the outlet, not
verified tablet endurance; no tablet model is picked. Basis per figure: MAKER (sheet, page), NETLIST, MODELED, INFERRED
(method stated), ASSUMPTION (a figure no document gives), PROPOSAL (the session's setting). The author's analysis, AI
arithmetic; not a qualified review and not the independent check.

1. DOUBLE COUNTING: what PS-IDLE-SPEC's 42.8 W holds of the tablet and the outlet
   pwr_budget.out's 39 PS-IDLE-SPEC shares name no tablet, USB-C, outlet or PDO row (none found); load_trace.out: the tablet has
   no row (yes) and the outlets are off in every state (yes); pwr_budget.py carries the outlet stage as node PDO (15.0 V, its
   efficiency 0.93 DECLARED: 'USB-C PD outlet stage, LM5176 behind TPS25740A: NOT PLOTTED, declared floor (gen_sch_a.py:978-980)') for the section 7.1 variants only.
   So the 42.8 W carries no tablet energy, no outlet conversion loss and no idle draw of the outlet converter: every
   figure below is ADDED to it, and nothing in it is counted twice
   THE OUTLET CONVERTER'S OWN DRAW WHEN ENABLED, NOT IN THE 42.8 W (board A: U19 LM5176 with VIN and BIAS on VBAT, MODE
   strapped to forced CCM by R135, RT R78 = 206 kHz, four CSD18510Q5B, L11 6.8uH XAL1010-682ME; U18 TPS25740A with VDD on +5V_DEV; NETLIST
   v2/ecad/pcb-a-power-a23/out/pcb-a-power.net sha256/16 6c40250c47195ebb):
     the LM5176's VIN operating current (not switching, VFB = 0.9 V) 2 / 4 mA typ / max (MAKER v2/vendor/ti/lm5176-datasheet.pdf p.6): 0.029 / 0.058 W
     the gate drive, Qg x fsw x VBAT a FET (VCC is regulated from BIAS = VBAT, so the gate charge is drawn at VBAT): Qg at
     VCC's 7.35 V (MAKER p.6) interpolated between the sheet's 58 / 75 nC at 4.5 V and 118 / 153 nC at 10 V typ / max (MAKER
     v2/vendor/battery/ti-csd18510q5b.pdf p.3): 89 / 115 nC; all four switching (buck-boost, the 15 V contract from a 12 to 16.8 V node) 1.057 / 1.370 W;
     two switching (buck, the 5 and 9 V contracts) 0.529 / 0.685 W (INFERRED)
     the TPS25740A's operating current while a sink is attached 1 / 3 mA typ / max (MAKER v2/vendor/ti/ti-tps25740.pdf p.8, I(SUPP)) at 5 V over
     +5V_DEV's converter at 0.90 (INFERRED): 0.006 / 0.017 W
     IDLE DRAW, ENABLED AND NOT LOADED: 1.09 W typ, 1.44 W max (INFERRED from the sheets' terms). Forced CCM keeps switching
     at no load; the inductor's ripple and core losses and the FETs' switching of the ripple are not in the sheets held, so
     the true idle draw is higher by an amount not quantified here. The budget below takes 1.44 W

2. THE PROPOSED USB-C SERVICE BUDGET (a PROPOSAL, measurable at the outlet; not a tablet's verified endurance)
   the class, 8 to 10 inch rugged tablets (REQ-011), from makers' spec sheets held back and cited in v2/vendor/sources.txt:
     Zebra ET40/ET45 (sha256/16 59f0c2807fb5b24d, p.3): 8 in. 6100 mAh 3.87 V, 23.61 Wh; 10 in. 7600 mAh 3.87 V, 29.41 Wh (MAKER)
     Samsung Galaxy Tab Active5, 8 in. (sha256/16 f5a40178566ca410, p.2): 5,050 mAh typical, rated (minimum) 4,900 mAh; no voltage or Wh
     printed (MAKER). At a 3.8 to 3.9 V cell nominal it is about 19 Wh (INFERRED, not a maker figure)
   THE OUTLET AS DRAWN (NETLIST, U18 TPS25740A): HIPWR and PSEL straight to DVDD, EN9V to GND, PCTRL to VAUX, so by the
     maker's Tables 2, 3 and 5 (v2/vendor/ti/ti-tps25740.pdf pp.28 to 29) it advertises 5, 9 and 15 V at 3 A, 45 W peak,
     with no way to lower it in the field. Its enable PD_EN is U26's AND of PD_SW_EN (an output of U28, the PCA9555PW (0x24))
     and OUTLET_OK, so the converter can be switched by software on existing hardware. No INA226 monitors the outlet (6 on
     board A, on +13V8_PA, +54V_POE, +5V_DEV, +5V_S1, +5V_S2, +5V_S3): the kit cannot meter the energy it delivers there
   P_CAP, PEAK OUTPUT POWER: 18 W at the outlet. It is the maker's own setting for PSEL direct to GND with PCTRL low
     (Table 5, TPS25740A, HIPWR high: 5 V 3.0 A, 9 V 2.0 A, 15 V 1.2 A). It needs a STRAP CHANGE on board A, an
     electrical correction neither designed nor checked: PSEL from DVDD to GND, and PCTRL from VAUX to GND (or to a spare
     expander line, which gives 36 W with PCTRL high, for shore power). With it the board, not the tablet, holds the
     cap. The 45 W of the drawn straps is a peak capability the budget never assumes
   SCHEDULE: the outlet converter enabled only in one 2 h window a day by the expander line; the case is W13 (13 to
     15 UTC). W10 (10 to 12 UTC), W19 (19 to 21 UTC, after dark) and ANY (the converter on all day) are compared in
     section 5
   E_OUT, ENERGY DELIVERED AT THE OUTLET: at most 36 Wh a day = the cap x the window, a ceiling the strapped board and
     the schedule enforce without a meter. It covers one full recharge a day of the class's largest battery (29.41 Wh) with
     up to 22 % lost in the tablet's own charger (no sheet held gives that loss). The model charges the ceiling every day,
     at the cap for the whole window: the kit side at its bound, not a guessed tablet draw. Whether a given tablet takes a
     full recharge in 2 h at 18 W is for the named model's verification (REQ-011's open acceptance, SC-45)
   STARTING CHARGE: the tablet full at the mission's start; every day's window, the first included, delivers the day's
     full budget, so the kit's energy does not depend on the tablet's starting charge (it decides only the tablet's use
     before the first window)

3. THE CONVERTER'S LOSS AT THE BUDGET'S OPERATING POINT (INFERRED: a pixel reading of TI's TYPICAL curve)
   LM5176 v2/vendor/ti/lm5176-datasheet.pdf sha256/16 98191bec36d43771, p.9 Figure 6-2 'Efficiency vs Load': VOUT 12 V, fsw 300 kHz, L1 4.7 uH, the maker's circuit;
   read with curve_readings.py's frame and reader (pinned). The VIN 12 V curve (a ratio of 1, as the 15 V contract from
   the 14.4 V node): 0.50 A 93.9 %; 1.00 A 97.0 %; 1.50 A 97.9 %
   the VIN 24 V curve (a buck at 0.5, near the 9 V contract's 0.63): 0.50 A 85.6 %; 1.00 A 92.7 %; 1.50 A 94.5 %
   at the operating point, the cap's 18.0 W, 1.50 A at the plot's 12 V: the lower curve reads 0.945; the record's declared 0.93
   (pwr_budget.py PDO) is the bracket; the budget takes the lower, 0.930. Board A differs from the maker's circuit: 206 kHz,
   6.8 uH, CSD18510Q5B, 5 to 15 V out; the plot's curves are typical at 25 C
   ENERGY AT VBAT A DAY for 36 Wh at the outlet:
     in a 2 h window: 36 / 0.930 = 38.7 Wh (19.4 W for 2 h); the idle draw is inside the curve's loss while it delivers
     ANY, the same Wh with the converter on all day: 36 / 0.930 + 1.44 W x 24 h = 73.4 Wh (INFERRED, indicative: at about 1.5 W
     out on average the converter runs far below the plot's lightest point, 0.2 A, so its loss is taken as the curve's plus
     the idle draw; the kit cannot hold a daily energy limit in this schedule without a meter on the outlet)
   PEAK AT VBAT with the cap: 18 W / 0.930 = 19.4 W, on top of the kit's load for the minutes the tablet draws its peak

